// mgeHost64 — fog melts into the REAL sky, sampled, not modelled (tasks/lighting.md).
//
// The fog AMOUNT was never the bug. `fogEnd = max(0.875, weatherFogRatio * AboveWaterFogEnd) * 8192`
// with every weather's ratio <= 1, so fog reaches exactly 1.0 at or before the draw distance in every
// weather, at every height. The fog COLOUR was: every lit path ended with
//     result = lerp(gFrameData.fogColNear.rgb, result, In.Fog);   // ONE flat colour
// while the sky it is meant to melt into is a gradient. The two agree ONLY exactly at the horizon,
// and MW's palette makes the divergence large — clear weather is a horizon of ~(206,227,255) against
// a zenith of ~(95,135,215), so the horizon colour is ~70% BRIGHTER than the zenith. Anything rising
// above the eye's horizontal plane was fogged toward a colour brighter than the sky behind it, and
// the error grew with elevation angle: a tower or a mountain came out as a GLOWING CUTOUT rather than
// a silhouette.
//
// WHY THIS SAMPLES INSTEAD OF EVALUATING A DOME. v1 of this fix lerped toward
// `lerp(fogColNear, skyZenith, saturate(dir.z))` — sky.frag's VColSource == 3 branch — on the
// assumption that that branch draws the sky. It does not, and has not since SK4: the client retired
// the host-gradient dome and re-ships MW's OWN per-vertex atmosphere colours, so branch 3 is dead
// code and that expression is a documented-slightly-bright APPROXIMATION of a gradient nothing draws
// (renderprocess.cpp, the SK4 note). Measured in game: mountain 148 against sky 143 far/low, 122
// against 115 nearer/higher — the right sign, growing with elevation, exactly as an over-bright
// analytic dome predicts. Clouds compounded it, and no analytic dome can ever contain a textured
// cloud layer.
//
// So there is no dome function here at all. gSkyColor is a copy of the colour target taken right
// after the sky pass — which is drawn FIRST in the colour pass, depth off — so sampling it at the
// fragment's own screen position returns the exact pixel this fragment is covering. At full fog the
// surface does not approach the sky, it BECOMES it, pixel for pixel, clouds and moons and sun glare
// included. There is nothing left to keep in sync and nothing left to drift: the one failure this
// class of fix keeps having is a second definition of the sky, and sampling removes the definition.
//
// STILL SCOPED BY CONSTRUCTION, and this is the property to re-check when looking at it: a fragment
// at or below the eye's horizontal plane is covering sky the dome does not reach, where the copy's
// alpha is 0 and the helper returns fogColNear — bit-identical to the flat fog it replaced. Ground,
// roads, the sea and terrain viewed from above cannot move. Looking DOWN from a height is the
// negative control; if anything shifts there, the uv is wrong.
//
// Requires (include AFTER opaque.srt.h): gSkyColor + gFrameData.fogColNear + gFrameData.fogParams.
//
// ⚠ UNITS (step 6a). This helper mixes TWO independently-converted things and they have to agree:
// gSkyColor, the snapshot of what sky.frag drew, and gFrameData.fogColNear off the wire. Once the
// scene target is scene-referred the host lifts fogColNear on upload and sky.frag lifts its own
// output (scenecolor.h.fsl, class 2), so both arrive here in the SAME space and fogSkyTarget's
// composite is meaningful. Lift only one of them and the mismatch draws a visible line at the sky
// dome's lower rim — s.a is 1 above it and 0 below, so the two halves of `s.rgb + fogColNear*(1-s.a)`
// swap over exactly there. That was the horizon-band seam reported on the first 6a build.

#ifndef SKY_DOME_H
#define SKY_DOME_H

// The other half of applyFog(): ONE extinction for the water surface and for everything submerged
// under it. It lives in applyFog rather than in water.frag because the mismatch it fixes is between
// the water and the SEABED, and the seabed is drawn by opaque/statics/terrain — fogging only the
// surface would leave the two disagreeing exactly as they do today.
#include "waterfog.h.fsl"
#include "fog.h.fsl"      // mwFogAirShare() — the air/water ray split

// ORDER MATTERS, and getting it wrong is why v2 still read as a cutout at mid distance. Extinction
// applied to the fog TARGET only shows up once fog is complete — by which point the surface has
// already been replaced by the sky and there is nothing left to silhouette. Extinction belongs on the
// SURFACE, ramping with how much fog stands between it and the eye:
//
//     lit *= 1 - extinct·(1-fog)·coverage      // FIRST: sit in the fog as a dark silhouette
//     result = lerp(skyTarget, lit, fog)       // THEN: melt into the sky
//
// so a ridge darkens as fog closes in, holds as a silhouette through the middle distance, and only
// then dissolves. The darkening's contribution to the final pixel is fog·extinct·(1-fog)·lit — a
// parabola peaking at HALF fog with a quarter of the knob's value, which is why the knob's useful
// range is much larger than the old target-side one was.
//
// pixelXy = this fragment's SV_Position.xy.
//
// The uv scale rides gFrameData.fogParams.zw and NOT gShadowParams.screenAlloc.zw, even though the
// main-view sky copy is alloc-sized and the two agree there. gShadowParams is one cbuffer shared by
// every pass, while gFrameData is per-pass — and the water mirror binds its OWN sky copy, which is
// kReflectSize² and drawn with a full viewport, so it needs a different scale for the identical
// shader code. Publishing the bound texture's own inverse extent alongside the texture keeps the two
// impossible to mismatch. (fogParams.zw carried wind/cell-epoch, which the host reads CPU-side off
// the wire and no shader ever read — see the FrameData note.)
//
// gSkyColor is PREMULTIPLIED (rgb·a, a), so compositing it over fogColNear is the source-over
// `s.rgb + fogColNear*(1-s.a)` — an ADD, not a lerp. lerp(fogColNear, s.rgb, s.a) would scale the
// already-premultiplied colour by alpha a second time and darken every partially-covered pixel.
// a == 1 (the dome) gives s.rgb exactly; a == 0 (no sky drawn) gives fogColNear exactly.
float4 fogSkySample(float2 pixelXy)
{
    return SampleLvlTex2D(gSkyColor, gSamplerBilinearClamp, pixelXy * gFrameData.fogParams.zw, 0);
}

// The sky behind this fragment, composited over fogColNear and faded by the feature's strength.
// Strength rides fogColNear.w — a lane the client already ships as 0 and nothing read, so this cost
// no wire growth, no CBV growth and no IPC change. 0 = byte-for-byte the flat-fog image (the A/B),
// and that claim covers EXTINCTION too: the host zeroes skyParams.w whenever this strength is 0, so
// one knob still switches the whole feature off. It has to — the host also skips the sky snapshot at
// strength 0, so anything still reading s.a past that point is reading a stale frame's sky.
float3 fogSkyTarget(float4 s, float skyShare)
{
    float3 sky = s.rgb + gFrameData.fogColNear.rgb * (1.0f - s.a);
    return lerp(gFrameData.fogColNear.rgb, sky, gFrameData.fogColNear.w * skyShare);
}

// ⚠ THE SKY BELONGS AT THE FAR END OF THE RAMP, NOT SPREAD ALONG IT — user, 2026-08-13: "fog
// blending with sky is a distance of last percentiles where fog saturates. So sky blending to 1
// meter away objects is just wrong."
//
// The feature's claim is about SATURATED fog: at full fog a surface does not approach the sky, it
// BECOMES it, clouds and all. But the target was built at full strength for every fragment and then
// mixed in by `fogAir`, so an object at arm's length with 3% fog opacity received 3% of a fully
// sky-substituted target — real cloud structure, painted onto near geometry at whatever weight the
// ramp happened to give it. Clouds visible THROUGH a near object is that, exactly.
//
// This is not the flat-fog colour being wrong at short range; fogColNear is right there and always
// was. What is wrong is asking a distant-field substitution to also be the near-field one. So the
// sky's SHARE of the target now ramps with fog opacity and arrives only over the last stretch, where
// the substitution is the thing that was actually claimed. Below the knee the target is flat
// fogColNear — bit-identical to the pre-feature image, which is the same negative control the
// strength knob gives, now also reached by distance.
//
// A #define and not a lane, matching FOG_SKY_HORIZON_RAMP below: this is a shaping constant on a
// ramp, not a per-frame quantity, and every free lane in gShadowParams belongs to a different
// subsystem. ⚠ It therefore costs a HOST RESTART to retune (skydome.h.fsl reaches opaque/statics/
// terrain/alpha/multimap, none of which hot-reload) — move it to a lane if it needs dialling in play.
//
// MW's fog is LINEAR in distance, so a knee on opacity IS a knee on distance: 0.85 hands the sky the
// last ~15% of the ramp.
#define FOG_SKY_SATURATION_KNEE 0.85f

// `fogAir` is MW's scalar: 1 = clear, 0 = fully fogged. Opacity is its complement.
// At fogAir 0 this returns exactly 1, so "at full fog the surface BECOMES the sky" is preserved to
// the bit — the property this whole helper exists to deliver is untouched at the only place it was
// ever true.
float fogSkyShare(float fogAir)
{
    return smoothstep(FOG_SKY_SATURATION_KNEE, 1.0f, 1.0f - fogAir);
}

// What fog lerps toward, for callers that have no surface of their own (volfog's haze, the water
// fill past the mesh). These are FAR-FIELD by construction — there is no near geometry to protect —
// so they take the full share and are unchanged by the knee above.
float3 fogSkyColor(float2 pixelXy)
{
    return fogSkyTarget(fogSkySample(pixelXy), 1.0f);
}

// ...and the same target for a caller that DOES have a distance: the melt on water's reflection,
// which is the one fogSkyColor() site that is a distance fog rather than a far-field fill.
float3 fogSkyColorAt(float2 pixelXy, float fogAir)
{
    return fogSkyTarget(fogSkySample(pixelXy), fogSkyShare(fogAir));
}

// THE AERIAL EXTINCTION, as a multiplier — hoisted out of applyFog() so the water pass can apply the
// IDENTICAL curve instead of a lookalike. Returns 1 (no-op) at strength 0, at fog 0, or wherever
// there is no sky behind the surface. See the long note in applyFog for what `shape` buys.
//
// ⚠ WATER WAS BRIGHTER THAN LAND ALL THE WAY IN, AND MATCHED ONLY AT SATURATION — user, 2026-08-13.
// That is exactly this term's shape: both surfaces melt toward the same target on the same ramp, so
// they agree at full fog, but only the applyFog path was ALSO being darkened on the way there.
// water.frag never calls applyFog (it composes its own fog against a reflection), so it was getting
// the melt and not the extinction.
//
// ⚠ AND IT IS NOT A MISSING CALL — the gate would have zeroed it anyway, which is why this needs its
// own decision rather than a copied line. `skyBehind` is `max(s.a, upness ramp)`: the sea sits AT OR
// BELOW the eye's horizontal plane, so `upness` is negative and the dome was never drawn at those
// pixels, making both signals 0. The negative control that deliberately protects ground and terrain-
// viewed-from-above ("the sea ... cannot move at any strength") also catches the sea seen EDGE ON,
// where sky is precisely what is behind it. Water is the case that gate cannot express.
//
// So water passes skyBehind = 1 and lets `ramp` do the gating instead, which is sound for this
// surface specifically: the water at the far end of the fog ramp IS the horizon, by construction —
// the melt already asserts this by melting toward fogSkyTarget(), and the extinction refusing to
// assert the same thing is the inconsistency. Near water is protected by the ramp (~0), not by a
// geometric test. Known residual: a river at extreme distance with mountains behind it darkens
// slightly on a claim that is false there — but at that distance it is nearly all fog target anyway.
float fogExtinction(float fogAir, float skyBehind)
{
    float ramp = pow(1.0f - fogAir, gFrameData.timeParams.w);
    return 1.0f - gFrameData.skyParams.w * ramp * skyBehind;
}

// How fast "am I standing against sky" ramps in above the eye's horizontal plane, as a reciprocal of
// sin(elevation): 38 = full by ~1.5 degrees. Deliberately a soft STEP and not a gradient — a ramp
// spread over the few degrees a distant mountain actually subtends would paint a vertical gradient up
// its own silhouette, which is a worse artefact than the one this fixes. The softness exists only to
// keep the horizon from being a hard line.
#define FOG_SKY_HORIZON_RAMP 38.0f

// THE fog operation: extinguish, then melt. `fog` is MW's scalar — 1 = clear, 0 = fully fogged.
// worldPosRel = the camera-relative world position every lit path already carries.
// Every lit path calls this rather than writing its own lerp, so the ORDER above cannot drift back
// apart one shader at a time.
float3 applyFog(float3 lit, float3 worldPosRel, float2 pixelXy, float fog)
{
    float4 s = fogSkySample(pixelXy);

    // WATER FOG — the segment of this ray that runs through water (waterfog.h.fsl). Exactly (0, 0)
    // when the feature is off or the fragment is above the surface, so everything below is unchanged.
    //   .x = the fog factor,  .y = the SHARE of the ray that is under water.
    float2 wf = waterFogSample(worldPosRel);

    // TOTALITY, not tidiness. `pow()` of a NEGATIVE base is NaN, and a NaN then survives every
    // multiply and lerp below — including the multiply by an extinction strength of ZERO, so the
    // feature's own A/B knob could not switch it off and the whole path looked exonerated.
    // `fog` IS saturated at the vertices, but a non-centroid interpolator extrapolates past the
    // vertex range on a partially-covered MSAA pixel (shaded at the pixel centre, which on an edge
    // lies outside the triangle) — so fog came in above 1 on edge pixels only, and every triangle
    // edge in the near scene grew black NaN dots. Found 2026-08-01; the interpolators feeding this
    // are CENTROID now, and this line means the next one that is not cannot reach a pow().
    fog = saturate(fog);
    // ⚠ AND THE PLANE TEST THAT USED TO SIT HERE IS GONE — 2026-08-15. mwFogAir compared this
    // fragment's z against the drawn water mesh and stripped the whole air ramp below it. Every
    // spelling of that test drew a line along the shoreline, because every spelling had to decide,
    // per pixel, something that is true of only part of the pixel:
    //
    //   bias 0 / 3        stripped land the water does not cover        -> dark line
    //   bias 12           left covered land carrying its own ramp       -> bright line
    //   bias 4, hard      exact in depth, still per-pixel               -> 1px dark, at distance
    //   + footprint bias  systematically under-stripped by half a pixel -> bright line, at mid
    //   + coverage weight unbiased, but still a per-pixel guess         -> dark shoreline, at distance
    //
    // Five values and three shapes, all of the same defect: a test that has to GUESS how much of this
    // pixel the water will cover.
    //
    // ⚠⚠ AND THE STRIP THAT REPLACED IT IS GONE TOO — the whole `mwFogAirSplit` family, same day,
    // same defect one level up. It was a smoother guess, and a guess is what could not work: shading
    // runs ONCE PER PIXEL and writes one colour to every sample it covers, so at a waterline pixel
    // holding both dry beach and seabed there is no number that serves both groups.
    //
    // What removed the question was giving the water surface a different SOURCE. It is a BLENDED draw
    // now (tasks/forge-water-blend.md), so what is behind it arrives through the blend unit, PER
    // SAMPLE, and water.frag no longer re-fogs it. Nothing downstream is going to apply this
    // fragment's air segment a second time, so this fragment simply keeps it — all of it, by the
    // honest share, with no plane anywhere in the expression.

    // ⚠ THE AIR RAMP ONLY OWNS THE PART OF THE RAY THAT IS AIR. This is the line that makes the
    // surface crossing continuous, and leaving it out is what put a hard step there on the first
    // build — a step that arrived BEFORE the crossing, as the camera descended, which is exactly
    // where the C0 argument said nothing could happen.
    //
    // The v1 rule was "camera below: the water fog REPLACES the air model; camera above: compose
    // both", with the double-count over the submerged tail written off as short. It is not short
    // near the surface: as the eye descends, the submerged tail grows until it IS the whole ray, so
    // a fragment on the seabed was still being melted most of the way toward the bright sky colour
    // right up to the waterline — and then, one frame later, not at all. The approximation was
    // largest exactly where the model claimed to be exact, and the switch had to pay for all of it
    // at once. (It also reads as the whole screen dimming, because what vanishes is the SKY colour
    // that had been lifting every submerged pixel.)
    //
    // MW's fog is LINEAR in distance, which is to say a UNIFORM density along the ray. A uniform
    // density integrated over a fraction of the ray is just that fraction of the optical depth, so
    // the air ramp's opacity scales by the air share and nothing has to know where the boundary is:
    //
    //     opacityAir = (1 - fog) * (1 - waterShare)   ->   fogAir = fog + (1 - fog) * waterShare
    //
    // At waterShare 0 this is `fog` EXACTLY — not to within a rounding error, which matters because
    // it is the A/B (the `1 - (1-fog)*airShare` spelling of the same algebra is off by up to 2^-25
    // and would have made "feature off" merely close). At waterShare 1 it is 1, i.e. no air fog at
    // all, which is precisely the v1 submerged branch — so that branch is gone, not conditioned:
    // one continuous expression reproduces both of its endpoints and every state between them.
    //
    // ⚠ THIS IS NOW THE ONLY THING DECIDING IT, and the one earlier attempt at that failed for a
    // reason that has been fixed rather than argued away. On 2026-08-14 this share went in WITHOUT
    // its other half — water.frag kept melting the finished composite, which re-applied the air
    // segment the share had just handed to the seabed — and the reports were "the water surface is
    // not fogged as much as above land fog" and "close to water surface underwater objects are
    // getting too much fog brightness". Both are double-counting, from opposite ends of the water
    // share, and the response at the time was to replace the share with a hard gate (mwFogAirSplit)
    // so the seabed carried NO air fog for the surface to duplicate. That fixed the double-count and
    // bought the shoreline seam, because the gate had to be evaluated per pixel.
    //
    // ⚠⚠ THE OTHER HALF IS NOW REAL, so the share is back and the gate is deleted. water.frag is a
    // BLENDED surface and fogs only its own contribution; the seabed is the DESTINATION and fogs
    // itself. There is nothing left to double-count, and the geometrically exact answer is finally
    // the one that composes correctly.
    //
    // At waterShare 0 this is `fog` EXACTLY — not to within a rounding error, which is what makes an
    // above-water fragment provably unable to leak: waterPathLength returns exactly 0 for a ray with
    // both ends above the surface, so no shoreline pixel can lose any part of its ramp at any camera
    // height, grazing angle or distance.
    float fogAir = mwFogAirShare(fog, wf.y, length(worldPosRel));

    // ⚠ ...AND WHILE THE CAMERA IS SUBMERGED, MW'S SCALAR IS NOT AN AIR FOG AT ALL. It changes
    // MEANING at the surface, which no share can express, and that is why this needs a frame test
    // where the line above deliberately needs none.
    //
    // distantland.cpp:763 swaps the whole range the instant the eye goes under:
    //     fogStart = DL.BelowWaterFogStart   (default -0.5 cells = -4096 units)
    //     fogEnd   = DL.BelowWaterFogEnd     (default  0.3 cells =  2458 units, ~35 m)
    // so `fog` stops describing atmosphere and starts describing WATER — the very medium this
    // feature models properly. Applying it to the AIR share is then wrong twice over, and both
    // errors land on the same pixels:
    //   - WRONG DENSITY. fog = (2458 - d) / 6554 is only 0.375 at ZERO distance and hits 0 by 35 m.
    //   - WRONG TARGET. What it melts toward is fogSkyTarget(), so an above-water object seen from
    //     below is 62% SKY at arm's length and 100% SKY past 35 m. Reported exactly that way: "I can
    //     see sky through objects".
    // It also drives the extinction ramp below (`pow(1 - fogAir, shape)`), so the same wrong scalar
    // was DIMMING everything above the eye's horizontal plane at zero distance — the third symptom,
    // and the one that looked like a lighting bug.
    //
    // A submerged fragment was already immune (wf.y == 1 makes fogAir 1), so this only ever touches
    // the case the share cannot see: the eye under, the fragment over. There the honest answer is
    // that we do not KNOW the atmospheric range — the client overwrote it — so the air segment
    // carries no fog rather than the wrong one. Conservative in the direction of visibility, and the
    // water column over the eye->surface segment is the dominant term anyway. Shipping the
    // above-water range alongside the below-water one would let this be exact; it needs a wire lane.
    if (waterCameraSubmerged()) { fogAir = 1.0f; }   // GEOMETRIC, not the feature toggle

    // EXTINCTION (skyParams.w), on the surface, ramped by the fog between it and the eye. The
    // standard lerp already carries the energy-conserving amount of extinction; this is deliberately
    // EXTRA, because "melts perfectly into the sky" and "reads as a silhouette on the way there" are
    // different asks and only the second one survives being looked at.
    //
    // IS THERE SKY BEHIND ME — two signals, and the extinction needs the UNION of them:
    //   s.a   is exact wherever the host sky pass drew: 1 under the dome, 0 elsewhere.
    //   dir.z is geometric, and covers the band between the dome's lower rim and the horizon where the
    //         host draws NOTHING at all (the target is cleared transparent, the host does not
    //         replicate MW's own sky clear, and MW's flat fog band shows through the composite there).
    //         s.a is 0 in that band even though sky is exactly what is behind you — and that band is
    //         where distant mountains stand, so gating on s.a alone put the extinction everywhere
    //         EXCEPT the one place it was asked for. Confirmed in play: "extinction applies above
    //         horizon band" and nowhere below it.
    //
    // The union keeps the negative control intact, which is the reason to bound it this way rather
    // than dropping the gate: BOTH signals are zero at and below the eye's horizontal plane, so the
    // ground, the sea and terrain viewed from above still cannot move at any strength. It also keeps
    // the exact signal in charge wherever it exists — the geometric one only fills the hole.
    //
    // AND THE GEOMETRIC ONE IS EXTERIOR-ONLY, enforced by the host publishing skyParams.w = 0 in
    // interiors (forgerender.cpp, the dp[63] write). It is a GUESS that sky is behind anything above
    // the eye, which is a fair guess under an open sky and a false one in a room: indoors s.a is 0
    // everywhere and correctly so, leaving the guess unopposed, and a step that completes in ~1.5
    // degrees about the eye's horizontal plane then draws a LEVEL LINE across every fogged wall —
    // darkened above, untouched below, tracking the camera, on surfaces only. That was a real
    // artifact (2026-08-01), and it is why the gate cannot be read as "sky coverage" without also
    // asking whether this frame has a sky at all. The steepness is deliberate (see the RAMP note) so
    // it cannot be softened away; the fix is to not ask the question indoors.
    //
    // rsqrt on a floored length² rather than normalize(): a fragment landing exactly at the eye would
    // make normalize() return NaN, and NaN survives every multiply and lerp below.
    float upness = worldPosRel.z * rsqrt(max(dot(worldPosRel, worldPosRel), 1.0e-12f));
    float skyBehind = max(s.a, saturate(upness * FOG_SKY_HORIZON_RAMP));

    // SHAPE (timeParams.w) — the knob that decides whether you get ONE hill going slightly dark and
    // then melting, or a stack of ridges in distinct shades.
    //
    // MW's fog is LINEAR in distance. A darkening that also ramps linearly with it therefore rises in
    // step with the wash toward the sky, so the two happen AT ONCE: by the time a ridge is dark enough
    // to read as a silhouette, most of it has already been replaced by sky, and the whole silhouette
    // phase is compressed into the last stretch before the fog wall. Successive ridges then differ
    // almost entirely by how washed-out they are, which is a single axis, and they collapse together.
    //
    // Real aerial perspective layers because extinction is EXPONENTIAL in distance — each equal step
    // costs a fixed FRACTION of contrast, so ridge after ridge stays distinguishable instead of
    // converging. That behaviour is front-loaded: most of the darkening is spent early. `shape` < 1
    // reproduces it against a linear fog scalar (0.45 ~ the Beer-Lambert knee over this range),
    // saturating the darkening well before the melt takes over. Then the two run in SEQUENCE — darken
    // into a silhouette first, wash toward the sky second — which is the order that gives each ridge
    // its own shade. shape == 1 is the old linear ramp.
    //
    // The host clamps shape away from 0 (pow(0,0) is 1, which would slam full extinction onto
    // geometry at the eye).
    // fogAir, not fog: the extinction is a stand-against-the-SKY term, so it has to fade out on the
    // same schedule the sky melt does. It needs no water gate of its own — at waterShare 1,
    // fogAir is 1, `1 - fogAir` is 0 and the ramp is 0, so a fully submerged fragment is not
    // silhouetted against a sky it has no line of sight to. One quantity disarms both halves.
    // ⚠⚠ WATER FIRST, THEN AIR — THE NESTING IS THE ORDER LIGHT ARRIVES IN, and getting it backwards
    // produced two reports at once on 2026-08-15: "water surface is not fogged enough" and
    // "underwater objects are getting air fog".
    //
    // This used to read `waterFogComposite(lerp(target, lit, fogAir), ...)` — the air fog applied to
    // `lit`, and the WATER composite wrapped around it. waterFogComposite is
    // `lerp(behind, waterColor, wf.x)`, so in deep water (wf.x -> 1) it melted the air-fogged colour
    // the rest of the way to the water colour and ERASED the atmosphere entirely: a seabed carrying
    // no distance fog at all, sitting behind a water surface that had to account for the whole pixel.
    // Near the surface wf.x is small, nothing was erased, and the same fragment kept a full air ramp
    // it should not have had. One wrong nesting, two opposite symptoms.
    //
    // Light travels seabed -> water -> surface -> air -> eye, so AIR IS THE OUTERMOST MEDIUM and is
    // applied last. The water column attenuates and in-scatters over its own segment; the air ramp
    // then attenuates and melts that result over its own.
    //
    // ⚠ IT WAS INVISIBLE UNTIL THE STRIP WENT. `mwFogAirSplit` forced fogAir to 1 for anything the
    // water covered, which made the inner air lerp an IDENTITY — so the order could not be observed,
    // and water.frag applied the air segment afterwards on the finished composite, which is the right
    // order by accident. Removing the strip did not create this bug; it uncovered one that had been
    // sitting behind an identity operation.
    //
    // ⚠ ABOVE WATER THIS IS BIT-IDENTICAL, which is what makes the reorder safe to make in a shared
    // function: wf is exactly (0, 0) there, so waterFogComposite returns `lit` unchanged (its lerp is
    // by 0 and waterFogBlend early-outs on L == 0) and the two spellings are the same instructions on
    // the same values. Only fragments with water in front of them can move.
    //
    // The water half is a COMPOSITE and not a lerp, because a medium that scatters does two separable
    // things to what is behind it — attenuates it and adds to it — and only the attenuation is a
    // lerp. waterFogComposite reproduces the plain fade exactly at W8 strength 0 (it is handed the
    // lerp as its own legacy input).
    float3 behind = waterFogComposite(lit, worldPosRel, wf);

    // ...and now the air, over the segment between the eye and whatever interface it reached.
    // fogExtinction moved down here with it — for an above-water fragment nothing changed (it is the
    // same multiply on the same value), and for a submerged one skyBehind reads 0 anyway, so this
    // term is 1 and the move cannot alter it.
    behind *= fogExtinction(fogAir, skyBehind);

    // fogSkyShare(fogAir): the sky substitution arrives only where the fog saturates — see the knee.
    // fogAir and NOT `fog`, so the share follows the same air-only schedule the melt does: a
    // fully submerged fragment (fogAir 1) gets share 0, which is right twice over — it has no line of
    // sight to the sky, and the water fog owns that ray.
    return lerp(fogSkyTarget(s, fogSkyShare(fogAir)), behind, fogAir);
}

#endif // SKY_DOME_H
